Run this complete example on its own:

```typescript
type Measurement = readonly [unit: string, amount: number];
const reading: Measurement = ["litres", 4];
console.log(reading[0]);
console.log(reading[1] + 1);
```
The outputs are litres and 5. Named tuple labels document the positions; they are not object property names.

This is a separate complete example; replace the previous example rather than appending its repeated type declaration:

```typescript
type Measurement = readonly [unit: string, amount: number];
function describe(row: Measurement): string {
  const [unit, amount] = row;
  return amount + " " + unit;
}
console.log(describe(["metres", 6]));
```
Destructuring gives each position a local name. It prints 6 metres.

An array of tuples can hold many records. ReadonlyArray<ScoreRow> describes a list you must not change through this reference.

The array method map calls a function once for each element and collects its returned values into a new array in the same order. For example, amounts.map(amount => amount + 1) returns an incremented value for each amount without changing the original list. For tuple rows, the callback can read row[0] and row[1], or use the destructuring already shown, to form each new label.

```typescript
const amounts = [2, 5];
console.log(amounts.map(amount => amount + 1));
console.log(amounts);
```
The arrays displayed are [3, 6] and [2, 5]. The callback receives each amount; map collects its returned numbers into a different array.

